    "MEANING\n",
    "2x2 Table:\n",
    "F2 = unit came from factory 2\n",
    "SB = unit fitted with supplier B coils\n",
    "Leaked Units = number of units in the population that had a leak\n",
    "Units = number of total units\n",
    "Rate = how many units leaked\n",
    "Lo Hi = 95% confidence interval\n",
    "First row shows that if the unit wasn't built in Factory 2 AND didn't use supplier B coils, it didn't have a leak issue\n",
    "Other three rows all overlap with rates between 54% and 57%. Since they are all so similar the best explanation/model is the Either factor switch (A logistic regression (or Bernoulli model) with one binary indicator for “Facility 2 OR Supplier B.). This is because there are two risk factors but when both risk factors are present, the rate doesn't increase. Bottom line is, Among failing units, about 55% of those built at Facility 2 or fitted with Supplier B coils failed from a leak, compared with 0% (0 of 1,570) of units with neither.\n",
    "\n",
    "Candidate Table\n",
    "The lower the mean, the more accurate the model fits the data. it shows that the either-factor switch is the best model. Interaction is also a very good model however it has extra noise that reduces its accuracy. \n",
    "\n",
    "Independence Statement\n",
    "If they were independent the rate would be around .794 but since it stays at 0.573 when both cases are true, the are dependent\n",
    "\n",
    "Other variabel dependence\n",
    "Since all p values are above 0.17, it shows that the leaks aren't majorly affect by customer region, product family, shift, or other variables. "
   ]
